course: dictionary
lesson: 4693
title: Homo: a genus, a prefix, and a word to avoid
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Homo is a science word, and it is also a prefix. A prefix is a small part added to the front of a word, and it changes the meaning. Homo- means «same», so a homophone is a word that has the same sound as another word.
explain: Scientists use Homo as the name of a genus, which is a group of close relatives. Humans belong to it. «The first humans like us, Homo sapiens, lived in Africa about 300,000 years ago.» Sapiens is Latin for «wise», so Homo sapiens means «wise human».
explain: Some words in the Homo group are no longer alive, such as Homo erectus and Homo neanderthalensis (the Neanderthals). Context helps you work out their meaning: the sentence says the first humans like us lived long ago, so Homo sapiens is our species, and it is the one that survives today.
fact: A prefix is a small part added to the front of a word, and it changes the meaning.
fact: Homo- is a prefix that means «same», as in homophone (same sound) and homogeneous (all of the same kind).
fact: In science, Homo is the genus that includes humans and our extinct close relatives.
fact: Homo sapiens is the species name for humans, and it is Latin for «wise human».
fact: Homo is also an old insulting slur for gay people. It is hurtful, so never use it.
exercise: «The first humans like us, Homo sapiens, lived in Africa about 300,000 years ago.» What does Homo sapiens mean? | Ancient rock | *Wise human | Ocean animal | Same kind
exercise: Homophones like «there» and «their» are a special kind of word. What does homo- mean in homophone? | Human | Old | Loud | *Same
exercise: Which of these is a species of the genus Homo? | Tyrannosaurus | *Homo erectus | Sabre-toothed cat | Mammoth
test: Homogeneous means what? | Mixed up with lots of kinds | Giving out light | Very old | *All of the same kind
test: Which is the species name for humans? | Homo erectus | Homophone | Neanderthal | *Homo sapiens
exam: What does the prefix homo- mean? | Far away | *Same | Old | Human
